KZ2 6082: vblank ticks against guest vblank-handler calls, one call per tick on request, and the
walker woken when a handler meets the acquire it stopped on. The vblank ticker only marks a pending bit and the guest
handler runs when a guest thread next pumps (an HLE call, a timer sleep, a semaphore wait), so ticks that come before
one pump give one call; the PS3 runs the handler once per vblank. Killzone 2's handler (1.29 0x48F40, v1.01 0x48660)
counts down from 2 and writes GCM label 0x49 = 1 at zero; the frame end (1.29 0x486A8..0x486CC, in the user-command
handler's frame-end function 0x48580) emits ACQUIRE label 0x49 == 1, then RELEASE 0: the game's own 30 Hz gate on the
RSX. A lost call delays that gate's next opening. Counted always (ticks, calls, ticks lost to coalescing);
GCM_ACQ_STATS=1 adds them to patch 6081's [acq] line. GCM_VBLANK_EACH=1 (off by default until measured) makes one call
per tick, at most 4 per pump. When a drain stops on an acquire (GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE=1 only), the frame thread waits on its
wake event instead of a plain 4 ms sleep, and the pump kicks it when a guest handler it ran has met that label (a label
met between the stop and the wait ends the wait at once), so the walker resumes at the write as the RSX does.
Diagnostic plus switches; no default behaviour change (with GCM_SEMA_ACQUIRE unset no drain stops on an acquire).

